---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why WB — A workbench for fleets and agents"
  description="WB is a local control plane for multi-repo work: managed worktrees, fleet operations, and recoverable agent intent. Adjacent tools cover pieces. This is the combination."
  path="/why/"
>
  <section class="page-hero why-hero">
    <div class="container why-hero-inner" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow">Why WB exists</p>
      <h1>Not another sync script.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Git is excellent one repository at a time. Agent harnesses are excellent
        one session at a time. Monorepo tools are excellent inside one workspace.
        <strong>WB is for the messy middle</strong> — many clones, coordinated
        change, and work that has to survive an interrupted agent.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#install">Install WB</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/skills/">AI skills</a>
      </div>
      <p class="why-claim">
        Bold claim, said carefully: we have not seen this combination shipped as
        one local, open-source tool. If you have, we want to know.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container why-essay">
      <header class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">The gap</p>
        <h2>People already invent WB. Badly.</h2>
        <p>
          A shell loop for sync. A personal worktree convention. A sticky note
          with the original prompt. A spreadsheet of open PRs. A script that
          almost safely cleans merged branches — until it doesn’t.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="why-gap-grid">
        <article data-reveal>
          <h3>Git worktrees</h3>
          <p>
            Isolation without a fleet model. No shared task identity across
            repositories. No private journal of who asked for the work.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-reveal>
          <h3>Monorepo tooling</h3>
          <p>
            Powerful when the product lives in one graph. Quiet when your company
            is thirty GitHub repositories and a release wave.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-reveal>
          <h3>Agent harnesses</h3>
          <p>
            Great at running a model. Weak at durable local claims: clean
            <code>main</code>, recoverable prompts, and honest cleanup after
            the session ends.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article data-reveal>
          <h3>Sync utilities</h3>
          <p>
            Clone and pull at scale. Stop before guarded worktrees, dependency
            campaigns, CI audit, or agent-safe lifecycle.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container why-essay">
      <header class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">What WB joins</p>
        <h2>Four jobs. One local control plane.</h2>
        <p>
          Each piece exists somewhere else. The product is that they share one
          layout, one safety contract, and one place an agent can trust.
        </p>
      </header>

      <ol class="why-pillars">
        <li data-reveal>
          <span class="why-pillar-index">01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Fleet layout</h3>
            <p>
              Canonical clones stay clean and available. Feature work lives in
              managed worktrees under a stable home — not scattered checkouts
              that quietly become the new <code>main</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal>
          <span class="why-pillar-index">02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Coordinated change</h3>
            <p>
              One task can span repositories. Create, guard, summarize, clean up,
              rename, or abort with the same task identity — and refuse the
              unsafe path instead of improvising.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal>
          <span class="why-pillar-index">03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Recoverable intent</h3>
            <p>
              The original prompt and later steering are private local evidence.
              A successor agent can resume with <code>wb worktree log</code>.
              A human can inspect without leaking bodies via
              <code>wb worktree info</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal>
          <span class="why-pillar-index">04</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Propagation ops</h3>
            <p>
              Dependency waves, migrations, hooks, CI audits, and fleet verify —
              the unglamorous work that keeps many repos from drifting apart.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container why-split">
      <div class="why-split-copy" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Humble about neighbors</p>
        <h2>Adjacent tools. Different job.</h2>
        <p>
          WB is not trying to replace Git, Nx, Cursor, or GitHub. It sits beside
          them as the local workbench that keeps multi-repo agent work from
          becoming landfill.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="why-compare" data-reveal aria-label="How WB relates to adjacent tools">
        <div class="why-compare-row">
          <span>git worktree</span>
          <span>isolation</span>
          <span>WB adds fleet + journal</span>
        </div>
        <div class="why-compare-row">
          <span>Nx / Bazel</span>
          <span>one workspace graph</span>
          <span>WB spans many clones</span>
        </div>
        <div class="why-compare-row">
          <span>Cursor / Codex</span>
          <span>run the agent</span>
          <span>WB owns the checkout lifecycle</span>
        </div>
        <div class="why-compare-row">
          <span>gh / myrepos</span>
          <span>sync &amp; API</span>
          <span>WB keeps going after clone</span>
        </div>
        <div class="why-compare-row">
          <span>Graphite / queues</span>
          <span>PR policy</span>
          <span>WB is local-first evidence</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head center" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Fit</p>
        <h2>Useful every day. Not for everyone.</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="why-fit-grid">
        <article class="why-fit why-fit-yes" data-reveal>
          <p class="why-fit-label">Built for</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Many private or public repositories</li>
            <li>Cross-repo changes and shared libraries</li>
            <li>AI agents that must leave recoverable work</li>
            <li>Teams who treat dirty <code>main</code> as expensive</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="why-fit why-fit-no" data-reveal>
          <p class="why-fit-label">Probably overkill</p>
          <ul>
            <li>A single application repository</li>
            <li>No agent workflows and rare fleet change</li>
            <li>A monorepo that already is the product</li>
            <li>Wanting a hosted dashboard more than a CLI</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt why-demo">
    <div class="container why-demo-grid">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">What “different” looks like</p>
        <h2>A task you can explain later.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Create isolated work across repositories, keep the prompt, inspect
          without leaking it, and summarize the whole effort when you come back.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div data-reveal>
        <Terminal
          label="why the journal matters"
          status="local only"
          lines={[
            { kind: 'command', html: 'wb worktree create bots-e2e \\' },
            { kind: 'command', html: '  sneat-bots sneat-go \\' },
            { kind: 'command', html: '  --original-prompt-file ./prompt.txt' },
            { kind: 'success', html: '✓ two worktrees · private claim' },
            { kind: 'blank', html: '' },
            { kind: 'command', html: 'wb worktree summary bots-e2e' },
            { kind: 'output', html: '2 worktrees · branches live' },
            { kind: 'command', html: 'wb worktree info .' },
            { kind: 'muted', html: 'digests only · bodies omitted' },
            { kind: 'command', html: 'wb worktree log .' },
            { kind: 'accent', html: 'exact prompt for the next agent' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section install-section" id="next">
    <div class="container install-grid">
      <div class="install-copy" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Still a workbench</p>
        <h2>Try it where the pain already is.</h2>
        <p>
          If your day is already multi-repo and agent-shaped, WB is less a new
          ideology than a cleaner version of the scripts you already trust too much.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="install-options" data-reveal>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#install">Install WB <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/skills/">Give an agent the skills</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="https://github.com/sneat-dev/wb">Read the source ↗</a>
        <p class="install-note">
          Questions, corrections, or a tool we should credit — open an issue on
          <a href="https://github.com/sneat-dev/wb/issues">GitHub</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
